# The advanced reference for bare-metal settings. You do not need to copy this file: `pnpm start` writes .env
# (gitignored) on its first run in a terminal, and `pnpm start --setup` changes the port and who can sign in.
# Add a line from here to .env only for a setting setup does not ask about. Docker users: the container does
# NOT read this file; see docker/README.md.
#
# The server reads .env from its working directory when it starts, and a value here WINS over the same
# variable in your shell or set by a launcher. `pnpm start`, `pnpm dev` and `pnpm stack` all start the
# server from the repository root, so all three read this file:
#   - `pnpm start` runs the production server in the foreground (it sets NODE_ENV=production).
#   - `pnpm dev` runs the watched server and the vite client. It checks this file first and does not start
#     if the server would refuse it.
#   - `pnpm stack up` runs the dev stack detached. It sets single-user and dev-only secrets itself, so it
#     needs nothing here, but a key set here replaces its value. `pnpm stack up prod` and
#     `pnpm stack up-fg prod` run the production server (NODE_ENV=production).
# Every key is optional; the full schema with defaults is packages/server/src/foundation/env/index.ts.
#
# NEVER put AUTH_FALLBACK in this file — it is a launch-time decision (boot-fatal here, #301), and you do
# not need to: what an un-credentialed request gets is decided by AUTH_MODE. single-user → the owner (from
# a loopback socket only); local/oidc/forward-header → a 401, so SSO is mandatory. To override that for one
# launch, export it on the command line: `AUTH_FALLBACK=deny pnpm stack up-fg prod`.

# single-user (default) | local | oidc | forward-header. `pnpm start --setup` sets this and PORT for you.
#AUTH_MODE=single-user

# Who can reach the server. single-user has no login, so it listens on this machine only (127.0.0.1) and
# refuses a wider BIND_HOST. A login mode under `pnpm start` listens on every interface; set 127.0.0.1 to keep
# the LAN out when a tunnel on this machine (`tailscale serve`, `cloudflared`) is the only way in. Never put a
# proxy or tunnel in front of single-user (README.md, "Other devices, and the internet").
#BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1

# The names this server answers to. localhost, IP addresses (http://192.168.1.20:8788), this computer's own name
# and <name>.local always work. Put any other name people type in the browser here, comma-separated: a tailnet
# name, a tunnel or proxy hostname. A leading dot admits the name and every subdomain (.example.com), never a whole
# top-level domain (.com, .lan). The hosts in OIDC_REDIRECT_URIS are added for you. `pnpm start --setup` writes it.
# The server refuses every other name, so a web page cannot reach it through your browser by pointing its own
# name at this machine (DNS rebinding).
#ALLOWED_HOSTS=orbweaver.example.com

# Where the app keeps everything (the database under db/, assets/, users/, the generated secrets/, reports/,
# and the regenerable cache/). Relative to the checkout. Back it up as one unit, minus cache/.
#DATA_DIR=./data
# A data dir from an older layout is moved into this one on the first boot. Set this only when that boot
# refuses and names it: the legacy entries to leave where they are, comma-separated (e.g. import-reports).
#DATA_LAYOUT_SKIP=

# Secrets need no setup. Left unset, the server generates each one once under the data root
# (data/secrets/session_secret, data/secrets/credentials_key) and reuses it on every boot. Back them up with
# the database: without credentials_key no saved provider key can be read; without session_secret no local
# password or sign-in works — and a boot that finds one missing while the database still depends on it
# refuses to start. Set a value here only to supply your own; an explicit value always wins.
#SESSION_SECRET=
#CREDENTIALS_KEY=

# The session cookie follows each request: `Secure` behind an HTTPS proxy that sends X-Forwarded-Proto, and a
# cleartext cookie over plain http (http://192.168.1.20:8788), so a LAN sign-in works but anyone who can watch
# that network can copy the cookie and be you. The login screen says so. There is nothing to set here.

# Cloud models — or paste the key in Settings → Connections instead.
#OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-…

# A model server on a private address (Ollama, KoboldCpp, LM Studio, vLLM, …) is a connection whose host the owner
# admits: the connection's "Admit <host>" button, or this env floor (hosts, IPs, CIDRs or host:port). Unset, single-user
# admits loopback only; setting it replaces that default.
#PRIVATE_ENDPOINT_ALLOWLIST=127.0.0.1,192.168.1.50
